// firestore.rules
//
// Security rules for ClassMate's Firestore database.
//
// How to apply these:
//   Option A (Firebase Console): Firestore Database -> Rules tab ->
//     paste this file's contents -> Publish.
//   Option B (Firebase CLI): reference this file as "firestore.rules"
//     in firebase.json, then run `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules`.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Real multi-teacher collaboration (this phase)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Classrooms are now shared documents at classrooms/{classroomId} —
// any member listed in that document's `memberUids` array can read and
// write it (see services/memberService.js for the roles: owner,
// teacher, viewer). A teacher's list of accessible classrooms is a
// separate pointer collection, users/{uid}/classroomRefs, which only
// that uid can read, create, or update — see that match block's own
// comment for the one narrow exception to this on delete.
//
// What this enforces:
//   - Only a member of a classroom (their uid is in its `memberUids`
//     array) can read or write it at all.
//   - Only the classroom's stated owner (ownerUid) can create it in the
//     first place, or delete it.
//   - A teacher can only read/create/update their own classroomRefs and
//     users documents (used for the one-time migration flag) — never
//     anyone else's. The one exception: a classroom's real, verified
//     owner may also DELETE another member's classroomRefs entry, but
//     only for a classroom they actually own and only for an actual
//     member of it — needed so deleteClassroom()'s single atomic batch
//     (the classroom doc + every member's ref, all removed together)
//     can succeed for shared/co-taught classrooms, not just
//     single-member ones. See that block's own comment for the full
//     reasoning and why this can't be exploited beyond that.
//   - The old teachers/{uid}/classrooms/ location from before sharing
//     existed is kept read/delete-able by its original owner ONLY —
//     needed for the one-time migration in
//     services/workspaceService.js, which reads then deletes from
//     there. Nothing new is ever written to this location.
//
// KNOWN SIMPLIFICATION (see Phase 1 explanation in project history):
// `update` here does not yet distinguish "teacher" from "viewer" — any
// current member can write. A viewer being unable to write, and a
// teacher being unable to edit `members`/`ownerUid`/`memberUids`
// directly, are follow-up hardening for when the viewer role and
// invitations actually ship, not implemented yet.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// "Join a Classroom" (co-teacher self-service, via a shared code)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// PROPOSED — added alongside the client code that needs it
// (repositories/firestoreClassroomRepository.js's createJoinCodeMapping/
// getClassroomIdByJoinCode/addSelfAsTeacher), but not yet verified
// against a real Firestore project: this sandboxed environment has no
// real Firebase credentials, so every other rule change in this
// project's history could be tested end-to-end — this one could not.
// Please review and test this specifically before deploying, the same
// way you would for any other security-rule change.
//
// The problem this solves: a co-teacher isn't a member of the
// classroom they're trying to join yet, so the existing `allow read`
// rule above (member-only) can't help them find it, and a query across
// `classrooms` filtered by join code would be denied for the same
// reason. Two additions, kept as narrow as possible:
//
//   1. A new joinCodes/{code} collection — a tiny, low-sensitivity
//      lookup (`{ classroomId: "..." }`) that reveals only an opaque
//      classroom id for a known code, nothing about the classroom's
//      actual content (no student names, no scores, nothing). Readable
//      by any signed-in user; write-once (create, never update/delete)
//      so a code can't be silently repointed at a different classroom
//      after being shared.
//
//   2. classrooms/{classroomId}'s `allow update` rule gains a second
//      path: a non-member may write, but ONLY if the resulting document
//      still has every field identical to before except their own
//      uid being added to `memberUids` and `members` — they cannot
//      touch scores, students, settings, ownerUid, or remove/modify
//      any other member's entry. This is exactly why
//      addSelfAsTeacher() was written as a narrow, additive-only update
//      (arrayUnion + a single new map key) rather than a full document
//      overwrite: a rule permitting "add exactly your own uid" is
//      possible to write safely; a rule permitting "a non-member may
//      write anything" is not.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Student join codes (no student authentication, by design)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Students join with no sign-in, no PIN, no password (see this
// project's CHANGELOG for the architecture discussion) — the
// classroom's student-facing code is the only thing standing between
// "anyone" and the roster. That means, unlike joinCodes above:
//   - studentJoinCodes/{code} must be readable with NO auth at all
//     (request.auth is null for every student device).
//   - Once resolved, the classroom document itself must also be
//     readable with no auth — Firestore has no way to say "readable
//     only if you arrived via a valid code," so the code itself (an
//     unguessable 6-character string) is the actual gate, the same way
//     a classroom's own Firestore document ID being unguessable is
//     what makes this safe at all, not authentication.
//   - WRITES stay restricted to authenticated members/owner, with one
//     narrow exception below for marking a student as joined.

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /classrooms/{classroomId} {
      // Split into get/list rather than one blanket `read`, verified
      // against actual usage: every access anywhere in this app's
      // repository code is doc(db, 'classrooms', classroomId) — a
      // direct read by a known ID — never a collection query. So
      // `get` needs to support the no-auth student flow (see below),
      // but there is no legitimate reason for `list` to ever succeed
      // for anyone, member or not — nothing in the app queries this
      // collection broadly, and permitting it would let an
      // unauthenticated client enumerate/scan classroom documents
      // instead of only fetching one they already have the ID or
      // student code for.
      allow get: if (request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in resource.data.memberUids) || true;
      // The `|| true` above is deliberate, not a mistake — see the
      // "Student join codes" comment block below: gets must be open to
      // unauthenticated students once they have a classroomId, which
      // in practice only ever happens via an unguessable code. Left
      // as two conditions rather than a bare `allow get: if true` so
      // the intent (member OR anyone-with-the-id) stays legible.
      allow list: if false;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid == request.resource.data.ownerUid &&
        request.auth.uid in request.resource.data.memberUids;

      allow update: if request.auth != null && (
        request.auth.uid in resource.data.memberUids ||
        isSelfOnlyJoin(request, resource)
      );

      allow delete: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid == resource.data.ownerUid;
    }

    match /joinCodes/{code} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;
      allow create: if request.auth != null;
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    match /studentJoinCodes/{code} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create: if request.auth != null;
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // NOT YET SUPPORTED: markStudentJoinedPortal() in workspaceService.js
    // needs an unauthenticated student device to flip one nested flag
    // (a student's hasJoinedPortal, buried inside teams[].students[])
    // — and there's no safe way to write a rule permitting "change
    // exactly this one deeply-nested field, nothing else" the way
    // isSelfOnlyJoin() does for the simpler top-level members/
    // memberUids case below. Writing a rule permissive enough to allow
    // this would also allow an unauthenticated visitor to rewrite
    // scores, students, or anything else in the document — not an
    // acceptable trade. Left deliberately unaddressed here rather than
    // either silently broken or unsafely open — see this file's
    // accompanying explanation for the recommended fix (restructuring
    // this flag to a top-level field, which a rule CAN safely permit).

    match /users/{uid} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;

      match /classroomRefs/{classroomId} {
        allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;

        allow create, update: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;

        // DELETE gets one narrow second path beyond "this is my own
        // ref": firestoreClassroomRepository.deleteClassroom() removes
        // the classroom document AND every member's classroomRefs
        // entry in ONE atomic batch (see that function's own comment).
        // A batch is all-or-nothing, so without this, the classroom
        // owner deleting anyone else's ref in that same batch would
        // fail the whole commit with PERMISSION_DENIED the instant a
        // classroom has more than one member — confirmed directly as
        // the real cause of a real failed deletion, via temporary
        // diagnostic logging before this fix existed.
        //
        // isClassroomOwnerRemovingMemberRef() below trusts nothing the
        // client claims: it reads the classroom document's OWN,
        // real ownerUid/memberUids fields directly. Deliberately
        // scoped by BOTH conditions, not just the first:
        //   - request.auth.uid == classroom.ownerUid — caller must be
        //     THIS exact classroom's real, verified owner, not merely
        //     signed in.
        //   - uid in classroom.memberUids — the ref being deleted
        //     (path segment `uid`) must belong to an actual member of
        //     THAT SAME classroom, so an owner can never use this to
        //     delete some unrelated user's unrelated ref under a
        //     classroomId that happens to match one they own.
        // Ordinary (non-owner) members are completely unaffected —
        // they still only ever satisfy the FIRST branch of `allow
        // delete` below (their own uid), exactly as before this
        // change; this function is never reached for them at all
        // unless they also happen to be the owner.
        //
        // Why get() here is safe even mid-deletion: Firestore
        // evaluates every rule within one commit (batch or
        // transaction) against a single, consistent snapshot of the
        // database as it stood BEFORE that commit's own writes are
        // applied — never against another write from the SAME commit.
        // So even though this exact batch also deletes
        // classrooms/{classroomId} itself, this get() still sees that
        // document fully intact, with its real ownerUid/memberUids,
        // not as already-gone. (If the classroom document genuinely
        // doesn't exist at all — e.g. an orphaned ref with nothing to
        // check against — accessing .data on a nonexistent get()
        // result fails this function's own evaluation, which Firestore
        // treats as this function returning false: falls through to
        // needing the first branch instead, never an open/ambiguous
        // allow.)
        function isClassroomOwnerRemovingMemberRef() {
          let classroom = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data;
          return request.auth.uid == classroom.ownerUid && uid in classroom.memberUids;
        }

        allow delete: if request.auth != null && (
          request.auth.uid == uid || isClassroomOwnerRemovingMemberRef()
        );
      }
    }

    // This rule's own create/update condition no longer
    // cross-references classrooms/{classroomId}/studentAuthLinks —
    // that collection, and the enrollment step that populated it,
    // were rolled back (see project history). A student write is
    // now authorized by a direct match between the document's own
    // uid field and the caller's real request.auth.uid instead — see
    // this rule's own comment below for the exact condition.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/studentGoals/{goalId} {
      // Readable by the goal's own linked student, or any teacher
      // member of this classroom.
      //
      // Checks resource.data.uid directly, NOT a studentAuthLinks
      // get() keyed by resource.data.studentId (an earlier version
      // of this rule did, and Firestore denied every list query
      // against this collection outright as a result — confirmed
      // directly: Firestore cannot prove a list operation satisfies a
      // rule that depends on another document's own fields, resolved
      // per potential result, and refuses the whole query rather than
      // risk it. uid is denormalized onto each document at write time
      // instead (see firestoreStudentGoalsRepository.js's own
      // submitGoal()), where a single-document get() against
      // studentAuthLinks is always safe — removing the dependency
      // from the read path entirely. The teacher-check get() below,
      // against classrooms/$(classroomId), is unaffected: classroomId
      // is this rule's own static path variable, not a per-document
      // field, so Firestore can prove it holds for every possible
      // result of the query.
      allow read: if request.auth != null && (
        request.auth.uid == resource.data.uid ||
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids
      );

      // A student may create ONLY their own goal — proven via a
      // direct match between the document's own uid field and the
      // caller's real, authenticated request.auth.uid (not a
      // client-supplied studentId taken at face value). Deliberately
      // does not cross-reference a studentAuthLinks mapping — that
      // collection, and the enrollment step that populated it, were
      // rolled back (see project history); this is the minimum,
      // direct check that keeps student goal writes working without
      // it. May only ever create with status 'pending_approval' —
      // never 'approved'. Deliberately NOT broadened for this
      // milestone's own completion-toggle feature — that write is an
      // update to an already-existing, already-approved goal, never
      // a create; see allow update below for its own, separate rule.
      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid &&
        request.resource.data.status == 'pending_approval';

      // Four, deliberately separate ways a studentGoals document may
      // be updated — kept as an explicit allow update (not folded
      // into allow create, update above) so each grant stays legible
      // on its own, per explicit product decision:
      allow update: if request.auth != null && (
        // 1) A student may keep editing their own goal's own text
        // while it's still pending — unchanged from this rule's own
        // original create/update condition.
        (
          request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid &&
          request.resource.data.status == 'pending_approval'
        ) ||
        // 2) A teacher member may approve — but ONLY flip status to
        // 'approved', touching no other field on this document.
        // Unchanged.
        (
          request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids &&
          request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['status']) &&
          request.resource.data.status == 'approved'
        ) ||
        // 3) A teacher member may request changes instead of approving
        // -- flipping status to 'changes_requested' and attaching
        // teacherFeedback (the text the student sees), touching no
        // other field. Mirrors branch 2 exactly (memberUids check +
        // affectedKeys().hasOnly(...) + one status equality) -- this
        // is deliberately the ONLY shape used, matching every other
        // scoped-update branch in this file. An earlier version of
        // this branch additionally required
        // request.resource.data.teacherFeedback.text is string;
        // removed because it was the one structural element with no
        // precedent anywhere else in this file (every other
        // hasOnly()-scoped branch validates only affectedKeys() plus
        // a top-level value equality, never a chained nested-field
        // type check in the same clause), and it's not needed for
        // security here: hasOnly() already guarantees teacherFeedback
        // is the only extra field a teacher can touch, and its
        // shape/type is enforced by this app's own single writer
        // (firestoreStudentGoalsRepository.js's own requestChanges()),
        // not by this rule.
        (
          request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids &&
          request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['status', 'teacherFeedback']) &&
          request.resource.data.status == 'changes_requested'
        ) ||
        // 4) The goal's own linked student may toggle daily
        // completion on their own, ALREADY-approved goal — proven
        // against resource.data.uid (the document's own, existing,
        // already-verified owner), never a client-supplied value;
        // request.resource.data.uid == resource.data.uid additionally
        // guarantees this same write can't smuggle in a uid change
        // alongside the completion fields. Scoped to exactly the five
        // completion-related fields via affectedKeys().hasOnly(...) —
        // status, text, uid, and every other field remain exactly as
        // untouchable here as they are in branch 1/2 above.
        (
          request.auth.uid == resource.data.uid &&
          request.resource.data.uid == resource.data.uid &&
          resource.data.status == 'approved' &&
          request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(
            ['completions', 'completedToday', 'currentStreak', 'longestStreak', 'overallCompletionPercent']
          )
        )
      );

      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    // Student notification read state (Stage 1 of the notification
    // architecture audit — see that audit's own Section F)
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // classroom.studentEvents itself (the event CONTENT) is untouched
    // by this block — still teacher-owned, still written only via a
    // full classroom-document update gated on memberUids above, same
    // as it always has been. This collection holds ONLY which of a
    // student's own events they've already seen — the one piece of
    // this feature a student genuinely needs to write themselves, and
    // the one piece that's safe for them to: unlike a teacher
    // notification's createdByUid/content, "have I personally looked
    // at my own event yet" carries no trust implication for anyone
    // else in the classroom. Deliberately NOT gated on memberUids (a
    // student's own identity structurally can never satisfy that) and
    // NOT modeled on feedPosts'/studentGoals' own uid == request.auth.uid
    // create checks either — this is simpler than either: the exact
    // same "your own uid, nothing else" shape users/{uid} already uses
    // globally, just scoped per classroom instead.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/studentEventReadState/{studentUid} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == studentUid;
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    // Class Feed
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // Mirrors studentGoals' own, already-proven pattern exactly: a
    // student writes only as themselves (request.resource.data.uid ==
    // request.auth.uid), never a client-supplied studentId taken at
    // face value.
    //
    // HONEST NOTE, not a new weakening: `allow read` here is
    // deliberately broader than studentGoals' own read rule — any
    // classmate genuinely needs to see every post in a shared feed,
    // which studentGoals never required. This is real, and worth its
    // own explicit acknowledgment: without a studentAuthLinks-style
    // mapping (removed earlier in this project's history), no rule
    // anywhere in this file — including studentGoals' own existing
    // create rule — can cryptographically prove a given anonymous uid
    // genuinely belongs to a real student in THIS classroom, as
    // opposed to some other classroom's own anonymous device. Feed
    // posts inherit exactly the same trust level every other
    // student-authored collection in this file already has today;
    // this is not a new gap introduced for this feature.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/feedPosts/{postId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;

      // Any authenticated classmate may touch reactorUids/commentCount
      // on ANY post — reacting to or commenting on someone else's
      // post is the entire point, so this is deliberately not
      // author-restricted. Firestore rules can't cheaply verify a
      // student only adds/removes their own uid from the array
      // without significantly more complex logic; accepted as a
      // low-stakes, explicit MVP trade-off — the worst case is a
      // cosmetic miscount, not a privacy or security issue. A
      // teacher member may additionally flip removedByTeacher and/or
      // media, and ONLY those fields.
      allow update: if request.auth != null && (
        request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['reactorUids', 'commentCount']) ||
        (
          request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids &&
          request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['removedByTeacher', 'media'])
        )
      );

      allow delete: if request.auth != null &&
        (resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid ||
         request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids);

      match /comments/{commentId} {
        allow read: if request.auth != null;

        allow create: if request.auth != null &&
          request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;

        allow delete: if request.auth != null &&
          (resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid ||
           request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids);

        allow update: if false;
      }
    }

    // Scoreboard Archive — one immutable snapshot per reset, created
    // only by a teacher (classroom member) performing the Reset
    // Scoreboard action (see services/scoreboardArchiveService.js).
    // Deliberately allows no update or delete at all, at the rules
    // level: "the archive must be read-only" and "never overwrite the
    // previous archive" are enforced here, not merely by the app
    // choosing not to expose an edit/delete control.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/scoreboardArchives/{archiveId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // In-app teacher notifications (see models/Notification.js,
    // services/notificationService.js) — the teacher-facing
    // counterpart to studentEvents above, but its own dedicated
    // collection rather than a flat array on the classroom document
    // (see Notification.js's own header comment for why), which is
    // exactly why this needs a rule of its own here that studentEvents
    // never did.
    //
    // Deliberately NOT modeled on feedPosts' own create rule (any
    // authenticated identity whose own uid matches a `uid` field) —
    // that rule exists to let a STUDENT's own lower-trust, anonymous
    // identity author a feed post. A teacher notification must never
    // be creatable by that same lower-trust identity: a student's
    // per-slot anonymous auth uid is never added to any classroom's
    // memberUids (only real, Google-authenticated co-teachers reach
    // memberUids, via addSelfAsTeacher() — see the "Join a Classroom"
    // section above), so gating create on membership alone already
    // excludes every student identity by construction, with no need
    // for a separate, more complex allow-list of safe (type, payload)
    // combinations. This is a deliberate, narrower trust boundary than
    // feedPosts' own, per explicit product decision for this feature.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/notifications/{notificationId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      // createdByUid must be the caller's own real uid — never a
      // value the client can set to impersonate a different teacher —
      // and only an existing member of this exact classroom may
      // create one at all.
      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids &&
        request.resource.data.createdByUid == request.auth.uid;

      // Any member may mark it read for themselves — ONLY readBy,
      // mirroring feedPosts' own reactorUids update rule exactly (see
      // that rule's own comment above on why this isn't further
      // restricted to "only adds your own uid": Firestore rules can't
      // cheaply prove that without significantly more complex logic,
      // and the worst case here is exactly as low-stakes as it is
      // there — a member could mark another member's own notification
      // read for them, a cosmetic annoyance, not a privacy or security
      // issue).
      allow update: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids &&
        request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['readBy']);

      // Immutable log otherwise — same posture as scoreboardArchives
      // above: a notification is never edited or deleted, only read
      // and marked read.
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // Learning Programmes — ProgrammeSession history (see
    // models/ProgrammeSession.js, services/programmeSessionRepository.js).
    // Config-sized Learning Programme data itself
    // (classroom.learningProgrammes) lives on the classroom document
    // above and needs no rule of its own; this block covers only the
    // unbounded session-history subcollection.
    //
    // PROPOSED, NOT YET VERIFIED against a live Firebase project —
    // this sandbox has no Firebase CLI or live Firestore project to
    // test against, the same limitation already documented elsewhere
    // in this project's history for other rules additions. Please
    // review and test this specifically before deploying.
    //
    // Mirrors the existing classroom-membership convention exactly
    // (see the scoreboardArchives block below, and feedPosts above):
    // any current classroom member may read or create a session.
    // Update is also member-scoped, since a session is genuinely
    // recorded incrementally over the course of a real occurrence
    // (attendance, then goals, then observations) rather than written
    // once — unlike scoreboardArchives, which is a one-shot immutable
    // snapshot. Delete is disallowed at the rules level, matching this
    // project's own historical-integrity invariants for Learning
    // Programmes: a session should never simply disappear.
    //
    // NOTE — classrooms/{id}/lessons/{lessonId} (Planner) previously had
    // no rule at all here and fell through to the deny-by-default
    // catch-all below; fixed below. classrooms/{id}/resources/{resourceId}
    // (Learning Hub) had the exact same gap — now fixed too, immediately
    // after the lessons block below, using the same member-scoped shape.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/programmeSessions/{sessionId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow update: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // Planner — Lesson (see models/Lesson.js, services/plannerRepository.js,
    // services/timetableLessonService.js, services/carryForwardService.js).
    // Fixes the exact pre-existing gap flagged in this file's own
    // history (see the NOTE above programmeSessions): this collection
    // previously had no rule at all and fell through to deny-by-default,
    // making Planner/Timetable persistence non-functional in production.
    //
    // create/update mirror programmeSessions' own member-scoped shape
    // exactly — only a current classroom member (a real, Google-
    // authenticated teacher) may attach a lesson plan, mark concepts
    // executed, or carry a concept forward. Update is member-scoped
    // (not narrower) because a Lesson is genuinely edited over its own
    // lifecycle by this feature — all ordinary teacher actions on an
    // existing document, not a one-shot immutable record the way
    // scoreboardArchives/programmeSessions' create-then-append pattern
    // is. Delete is disallowed at the rules level: this app never
    // deletes a Lesson once created (carrying a concept forward moves
    // it between Lessons; it never removes one).
    //
    // READ is deliberately broader than create/update — ADDED for the
    // Concept Feedback loop (see models/Lesson.js's own
    // getFeedbackEligibleConceptIds(), services/studentPortalDataService.js's
    // getConceptFeedbackForLesson()): a student, not a classroom member,
    // needs to read a specific Lesson (by the exact id a StudentEvent's
    // own payload points them to — see config/studentEventNavigation.js)
    // to see which concepts are actually awaiting their feedback. This
    // is not a new trust boundary invented for this feature — it's the
    // exact same "any authenticated identity, teacher or anonymous
    // student device, since Firestore has no cheap way to prove which
    // classroom an anonymous uid genuinely belongs to" boundary
    // feedPosts' and studentGoals' own read rules already accept
    // elsewhere in this file, applied here for the same reason. Lesson
    // content (a topic + concept ids) is no more sensitive than a Feed
    // post; a Lesson's own id is never guessable/listable (list stays
    // implicitly denied — no allow list exists here, same as every
    // other collection in this file), so this doesn't let anyone
    // enumerate or discover Lessons they weren't already pointed at.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/lessons/{lessonId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow update: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // Lesson Plan (Phase 2/3 — see models/LessonPlan.js,
    // services/lessonPlanService.js, services/lessonPlanReviewService.js).
    // PRE-EXISTING GAP FIXED IN PHASE 3: this collection had no rule at
    // all since Phase 2 shipped, the exact same "falls through to
    // deny-by-default" gap this file's own Resource comment (just below)
    // already documents for that collection — every read/write was
    // silently non-functional against a real deployed project until now.
    //
    // PHASE 3 HARDENING (this version): the first Phase 3 draft of this
    // rule only checked the TOP-LEVEL `status` field on write, which left
    // three real holes, closed below:
    //   1. `create` didn't restrict the INITIAL status/reviewHistory at
    //      all — a client could create a brand-new document already
    //      marked `status: 'approved'` with a fabricated reviewHistory,
    //      skipping the entire review workflow via the create path
    //      rather than update.
    //   2. The author's own `update` branch didn't check `resource.data
    //      .status` at all — an author could keep editing lesson CONTENT
    //      after submitting (status stays 'submitted'), silently
    //      changing what a reviewer is looking at mid-review, something
    //      the UI (LessonPlanBuilderView.js's own `editable` gating)
    //      already prevents but the data layer did not.
    //   3. The reviewer's own `update` branch let them change ANY field
    //      as long as the status transition was legal — nothing stopped
    //      a compromised/malicious reviewer-role account from rewriting
    //      lesson CONTENT (topic, activities, etc.) under cover of
    //      "requesting changes."
    //
    // This version fixes all three by checking, per branch, exactly
    // which fields are allowed to change (`diff().affectedKeys()
    // .hasOnly([...])`, the same pattern this file's own isSelfOnlyJoin()
    // helper already established for classrooms) and validating the
    // shape of whatever gets appended to reviewHistory, matching
    // services/lessonPlanReviewService.js's actual behavior field-for-
    // field (its own nextRoundNumber()/closeCurrentRound() split — see
    // that file's header comment):
    //
    //   CREATE: only as 'draft', with empty reviewHistory/activeComments
    //     — a lesson plan can never be born already submitted/approved.
    //
    //   UPDATE, author branch (resource.data.createdByUid == the
    //     writer): only while resource.data.status is 'draft' or
    //     'changes_requested' — locked out entirely once SUBMITTED or
    //     APPROVED, closing hole #2. Two allowed shapes:
    //       (a) a content edit — status unchanged, reviewHistory
    //           byte-for-byte unchanged (nothing added, nothing edited),
    //           activeComments never GROWS (a content edit only ever
    //           prunes a deleted activity's own open comments, per
    //           lessonPlanService.js's deleteActivity()).
    //       (b) a (re)submission — status becomes 'submitted', exactly
    //           one well-formed SUBMITTED round appended (byUid ==
    //           the author, comments == [] — matching submitForReview()
    //           never creating comments of its own), activeComments
    //           fully cleared.
    //     Never 'approved' — that transition has no author-branch shape
    //     at all, so it's rejected by construction, not just excluded.
    //
    //   UPDATE, reviewer branch (any OTHER current member whose role
    //     grants the matching permission — REVIEW_LESSON_PLAN for
    //     'changes_requested', APPROVE_LESSON_PLAN for 'approved'; both
    //     currently granted to the same 'owner'/'teacher' roles in
    //     config/memberRoles.js, kept as two separate literal checks
    //     below anyway in case a future role split ever gives one
    //     without the other): only from resource.data.status ==
    //     'submitted', and ONLY status/reviewerUid/reviewHistory/
    //     activeComments/updatedAt may change at all — closing hole #3,
    //     a reviewer's write can never touch lesson content, full stop.
    //     Exactly one well-formed round appended, byUid == the reviewer,
    //     never the plan's own author (self-review blocked here too, not
    //     just in the service). requestChanges requires >=1 comment in
    //     that round (mirrors the service's own "a bare status flip
    //     leaves the teacher nothing to act on" refusal); approve clears
    //     activeComments entirely.
    //
    // DISCLOSED, DELIBERATE LIMITATION (not a silent gap): this does NOT
    // verify that reviewHistory entries OTHER than the newly-appended one
    // are byte-identical to before (only that the list as a whole grew by
    // exactly one, well-formed entry) — Firestore rules has no clean
    // "slice" operation to compare "everything except the last element."
    // A tampered client could in principle rewrite an EARLIER round's own
    // recorded text/byUid while otherwise satisfying every check here.
    // This is the same class of accepted simplification this file's own
    // classrooms rule already documents (see its KNOWN SIMPLIFICATION
    // note near the top) — flagged explicitly rather than silently
    // assumed solved. Full tamper-proofing of historical entries would
    // need either a Cloud Function-verified write path or per-round
    // subcollection documents (each individually immutable), neither of
    // which this phase builds — see the Phase 3 hardening report for the
    // explicit call to stop and review here rather than overreach.
    //
    // Verified against a real Firestore Rules Unit Testing emulator run —
    // see firebase-rules-verification/lessonPlans.rules.verify.js — not
    // just reasoned about; see that directory's own README for how to
    // re-run it.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/lessonPlans/{lessonPlanId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids &&
        request.resource.data.createdByUid == request.auth.uid &&
        request.resource.data.status == 'draft' &&
        request.resource.data.reviewHistory.size() == 0 &&
        request.resource.data.activeComments.size() == 0;

      allow update: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids &&
        (
          isLessonPlanAuthorEdit(resource, request) ||
          isLessonPlanReviewerAction(resource, request, get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.members[request.auth.uid].role)
        );

      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // Teaching Ideas (Phase 4 — see services/teachingIdeasService.js,
    // repositories/teachingIdeasRepository.js). A DELIBERATELY SEPARATE,
    // TOP-LEVEL collection — never a subcollection of `classrooms`, and
    // never sharing the `lessonPlans` rule above — because its whole
    // purpose is different: "APPROVED lessons are globally discoverable
    // within ClassMate" (explicit Phase 4 product direction) is a
    // completely different security boundary than "a classroom member
    // may read their own classroom's lesson plans." Reusing the
    // `lessonPlans` rule for this would have meant either exposing that
    // classroom-scoped collection globally (explicitly ruled out) or
    // leaving Teaching Ideas with no rule of its own — this fixes that
    // deliberately rather than by accident, matching this file's own
    // running theme of collections that must never fall through to
    // deny-by-default unexamined (see the Resource block's own comment
    // just below for the same lesson learned the hard way once already).
    //
    // read: any authenticated ClassMate user, unconditionally — this IS
    // the "globally discoverable" requirement. A document's own id
    // isn't guessable/listable in a way that matters here regardless,
    // since `list` queries are additionally scoped by the `conceptIds`
    // filter services/teachingIdeasService.js always applies (see
    // repositories/teachingIdeasRepository.js's own header comment on
    // why an unbounded "list everything" query isn't offered at the
    // service layer) — this rule doesn't need to (and structurally
    // can't cheaply) re-enforce that same scoping itself, since Firestore
    // rules for `list` apply per-matched-document, not to the query
    // shape a client chooses to send.
    //
    // create: the ONLY way content enters this collection. Required,
    // all together — this is where "approved-only," "same-classroom-
    // scoped publisher," and "content and provenance can't be forged"
    // are actually enforced, not just assumed from how the client-side
    // publish call happens to build its payload:
    //   1. The document's own id must equal its own claimed
    //      `sourceLessonPlanId` — prevents publishing plan A's content
    //      under plan B's id.
    //   2. The writer must be a CURRENT member of the classroom the
    //      projection itself claims as `sourceClassroomId` — a random
    //      unrelated authenticated user cannot publish an entry
    //      attributed to a classroom they don't belong to.
    //   3. The REAL source document at that exact classroom+id must
    //      ACTUALLY be `status == 'approved'` right now, verified via a
    //      fresh `get()` on the real `lessonPlans` document — never
    //      trusted from the write's own payload. A DRAFT/SUBMITTED/
    //      CHANGES_REQUESTED plan can never be published, no matter
    //      what the client claims.
    //   4. EVERY content field in the write must be byte-for-byte
    //      IDENTICAL to that same real source document's own field —
    //      see isFaithfulTeachingIdeaProjection() below. This closes a
    //      real gap an earlier version of this rule left open: checks
    //      1-3 alone only prove "a real, approved lesson exists at this
    //      id, and I belong to its classroom" — they say nothing about
    //      whether the CONTENT being published is that lesson's actual
    //      content. Without check 4, a classroom member with real,
    //      legitimate access to any one approved lesson could publish
    //      ARBITRARY globally-visible content (including a fabricated
    //      `teacherDisplayName`) under that lesson's real id — global
    //      readability makes this a materially worse exposure than the
    //      equivalent gap would be on a classroom-scoped collection.
    //      `teacherDisplayName` is checked against the real classroom's
    //      own `members[createdByUid].displayName` for the same reason
    //      — attribution is content too, not just decoration.
    //
    // update/delete: always false. Once published, a Teaching Idea is
    // permanently immutable — matches Phase 3's own "APPROVED is
    // terminal, no revision mechanism yet" design exactly, so there is
    // no "re-publish a changed approved lesson" path to secure or
    // forget to secure. If a future phase introduces approved-lesson
    // revisions, THIS is the line that will need deliberate revisiting
    // — not a gap silently inherited from today.
    //
    // Verified against a real Firestore Rules Unit Testing emulator —
    // see firebase-rules-verification/teachingIdeas.rules.verify.js.
    match /teachingIdeas/{lessonPlanId} {
      // NOTE: deliberately nested INSIDE this match block, not a
      // top-level file function like isLessonPlanAuthorEdit() below —
      // this one calls get() with a $(database)-relative path, and
      // `database` is only in scope inside the enclosing
      // `match /databases/{database}/documents { ... }` block. A
      // top-level function has no access to that binding at all
      // (silently resolves to a broken path, not a compile error),
      // which is exactly the bug this comment exists to prevent
      // reintroducing.
      function isFaithfulTeachingIdeaProjection() {
        let sourcePlan = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(request.resource.data.sourceClassroomId)/lessonPlans/$(lessonPlanId)).data;
        let classroomData = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(request.resource.data.sourceClassroomId)).data;
        return sourcePlan.status == 'approved' &&
          request.resource.data.conceptIds == sourcePlan.conceptIds &&
          request.resource.data.gradeLabel == sourcePlan.gradeLabel &&
          request.resource.data.subjectId == sourcePlan.subjectId &&
          request.resource.data.topic == sourcePlan.topic &&
          request.resource.data.lessonObjective == sourcePlan.lessonObjective &&
          request.resource.data.bigQuestion == sourcePlan.bigQuestion &&
          request.resource.data.swbatObjectives == sourcePlan.swbatObjectives &&
          request.resource.data.selfOthersIndia == sourcePlan.selfOthersIndia &&
          request.resource.data.assessments == sourcePlan.assessments &&
          request.resource.data.spark == sourcePlan.spark &&
          request.resource.data.activities == sourcePlan.activities &&
          request.resource.data.pairExplanation == sourcePlan.pairExplanation &&
          request.resource.data.finalQuestion == sourcePlan.finalQuestion &&
          request.resource.data.teacherLookFors == sourcePlan.teacherLookFors &&
          request.resource.data.teacherDisplayName == classroomData.members[sourcePlan.createdByUid].displayName;
      }

      allow read: if request.auth != null;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.resource.data.sourceLessonPlanId == lessonPlanId &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(request.resource.data.sourceClassroomId)).data.memberUids &&
        isFaithfulTeachingIdeaProjection();

      allow update: if false;
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // Learning Hub — Resource (see models/Resource.js,
    // services/resourceService.js, services/resourceRepository.js).
    // Fixes the exact pre-existing gap flagged in this file's own NOTE
    // above: this collection previously had no rule at all and fell
    // through to deny-by-default, making resource create/read/update/
    // delete non-functional in production for every caller — both
    // ui/views/ConceptWorkspaceView.js's own pre-existing "+Add
    // Resource" and the Timetable's own "+ Add resource".
    //
    // Same member-scoped shape as the lessons block immediately above:
    // read requires only a signed-in identity (a Resource's own id is
    // never guessable/listable — list stays implicitly denied, no
    // allow list exists here, same as every other collection in this
    // file); create/update require the caller be a current classroom
    // member. Unlike lessons (which never deletes), delete is also
    // member-scoped rather than disallowed — services/resourceService.js's
    // own deleteResource() is a real, existing, teacher-facing action
    // (see that file's own header comment on this app having no
    // separate "Unlink" vs "Delete" distinction yet), so this only
    // grants what that already-existing function needs, not more.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/resources/{resourceId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;

      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow update: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;

      allow delete: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;
    }

    // Learning Record — StudentConceptRecord (see
    // models/StudentConceptRecord.js, services/learningRecordService.js,
    // repositories/firestoreStudentConceptRecordsRepository.js). Phase N
    // migration: this per-student data used to live inside
    // classrooms/{id}'s own teams[].students[].learningRecord map,
    // which a real anonymous student device can never safely write to
    // (Firestore rules cannot diff a single element inside a nested
    // array/object — the same documented limitation behind
    // studentEntries/membershipLinks' own move to a subcollection).
    // This collection is the architecturally correct fix: a dedicated,
    // per-record document a student can be authorized against
    // directly. The classrooms/{classroomId} update rule itself is
    // UNCHANGED and UNBROADENED by this — a non-member still cannot
    // write to the classroom document at all, exactly as before.
    //
    // recordId is deterministic — `${uid}_${conceptId}`, keyed on the
    // WRITER'S OWN real auth uid, deliberately NOT `${studentId}_${conceptId}`.
    // A studentId-keyed id would be fully predictable in advance by any
    // authenticated device (roster student ids and concept ids are both
    // readable) — since Firestore evaluates any write to an
    // already-existing id as `update` (never `create`), a hostile
    // device could pre-create another real student's own record first,
    // permanently locking them out (update requires
    // request.auth.uid == resource.data.uid, which a squatted record
    // would never satisfy for its real, intended student). Keying on
    // uid instead means a device can only ever pre-empt itself, never
    // another real device — see
    // repositories/firestoreStudentConceptRecordsRepository.js's own
    // header comment for the full reasoning, including the accepted,
    // already-precedented tradeoff (the same student on two devices
    // gets two separate records, exactly like studentGoals already
    // allows today).
    //
    // Deliberately mirrors studentGoals' own, already-proven identity
    // pattern (checked directly, per Phase N's own investigation, as
    // the strongest safe pattern this codebase actually has): a
    // student writes only as themselves, proven by
    // request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid — never a
    // studentAuthLinks-style cross-check, since that collection (and
    // the enrollment step that populated it) was rolled back earlier
    // in this project's history (see the studentGoals rule's own
    // comment above) and no replacement exists. This is the same,
    // already-accepted trust level every other student-authored
    // collection in this file has today — not a new gap.
    //
    // Deliberately NO "claim an existing unclaimed record" path, unlike
    // an earlier draft of this rule: a record only ever comes into
    // existence via its own owning student's real create — there is no
    // teacher-create branch, and therefore no unclaimed/uid-less state
    // a later student write would need to "claim." This is possible
    // specifically because, per Phase N's own investigation,
    // setNotebookStatus()/resolveHelpRequest() (the only functions that
    // would ever need a teacher-initiated create) have zero real
    // callers anywhere in the app — no teacher write permission is
    // granted here at all, ahead of a UI that doesn't exist yet. If
    // that UI is built later, this rule needs a real teacher-create
    // branch added alongside it, not before.
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/studentConceptRecords/{recordId} {
      // Readable by the record's own owning student, or any teacher
      // member of this classroom — checks resource.data.uid directly,
      // not a cross-document get(), for the same list-query-provability
      // reason documented on the studentGoals rule above.
      //
      // resource != null is required here, not optional: Firestore
      // THROWS (not merely denies) when a rule accesses resource.data.*
      // against a get() for a document that doesn't exist yet — the
      // exact case a client checking "do I already have a record for
      // this concept" hits on its very first attempt. Discovered via
      // this project's own real-emulator E2E test, not by inspection —
      // repositories/firestoreStudentConceptRecordsRepository.js's own
      // findRecord() avoids ever triggering this (a query's rule is
      // only evaluated against documents actually returned, never a
      // nonexistent one), but this guard is kept here regardless as
      // defense in depth for any future getDoc()-by-id caller.
      allow read: if request.auth != null && resource != null && (
        request.auth.uid == resource.data.uid ||
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids
      );

      // A student may create ONLY their own record: uid must be their
      // own real, authenticated identity, and recordId must match
      // that same uid plus the document's own declared conceptId — a
      // client can't create e.g.
      // classrooms/{id}/studentConceptRecords/some-other-uid_concept-1,
      // and, combined with uid == request.auth.uid below, can never
      // land its own write on a document keyed by a different real
      // device's uid at all. classroomId must match this rule's own
      // path variable, so a record can't claim to belong to a
      // different classroom than the one it's actually stored under.
      //
      // notebook/helpRequested must be exactly
      // models/StudentConceptRecord.js's own createStudentConceptRecord()
      // defaults on creation — 'not_required' / false — since a
      // student is never allowed to set either field themselves (see
      // the update rule below) and there is no teacher-create path
      // that would ever need to set them to something else. understanding
      // is not further restricted beyond being present: this app's own
      // single writer (services/studentPortalDataService.js's
      // setUnderstandingForCurrentStudent()) already only ever sends
      // one of config/learningRecordConfig.js's own real
      // UNDERSTANDING_KEYS, matching this file's own established
      // convention of not re-validating a value's content in rules
      // when the app's own single writer already guarantees its shape
      // (see the studentGoals rule's own comment on teacherFeedback.text).
      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        request.resource.data.classroomId == classroomId &&
        request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid &&
        recordId == request.auth.uid + '_' + request.resource.data.conceptId &&
        request.resource.data.notebook == 'not_required' &&
        request.resource.data.helpRequested == false;

      // The record's own owning student may update ONLY the fields
      // they're actually allowed to control — understanding and
      // helpRequested (see services/learningRecordStudentService.js's
      // own setUnderstanding()/requestHelp()/withdrawHelpRequest(), the
      // exact same control boundary this rule enforces). classroomId,
      // studentId, conceptId, uid, and notebook are all untouchable
      // here: hasOnly() below already structurally guarantees none of
      // them can be part of this write's diff at all; the explicit
      // uid equality check is kept anyway, purely for the same
      // legibility reason studentGoals' own update branch 4 keeps its
      // own redundant uid check.
      //
      // No teacher-update branch exists — see this match block's own
      // header comment on why (setNotebookStatus()/resolveHelpRequest()
      // have no real caller today).
      allow update: if request.auth != null &&
        request.auth.uid == resource.data.uid &&
        request.resource.data.uid == resource.data.uid &&
        request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['understanding', 'helpRequested', 'updatedAt']);

      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    // Learning Circle Student Data Boundary — Phase 1: membership
    // identity foundation ONLY. See the security audit and
    // architecture design this Phase was authorized from (project
    // history) for the full reasoning; summarized here.
    //
    // WHAT THIS ESTABLISHES: one device's own per-slot anonymous
    // Firebase Auth uid, self-attested as corresponding to a specific
    // studentId's ACTIVE membership in a specific Learning Programme,
    // verified against that programme's own real membership data at
    // the moment of creation — not merely trusted at face value the
    // way a bare client-supplied studentId would be.
    //
    // WHY THIS DOCUMENT'S OWN ID IS THE UID, NOT THE STUDENTID: so a
    // FUTURE rule (StudentEntry, not part of this Phase) can resolve
    // "does the caller's own uid correspond to an active member of
    // this programme" via a single, concrete get() keyed directly by
    // request.auth.uid — never a query, never a search across many
    // documents, and therefore never subject to the exact limitation
    // already documented on the studentGoals rule above (Firestore
    // cannot prove a rule holds for every possible result of a LIST
    // operation when that rule depends on another document's own
    // per-result-varying field; a single get() by a value fixed for
    // the whole request has no such problem).
    //
    // WHY THE CREATE RULE CAN VERIFY ACTIVE MEMBERSHIP WITHOUT A
    // SEPARATE learningProgrammes SUBCOLLECTION: LearningProgramme
    // documents don't exist as their own collection today — they're
    // embedded in classroom.learningProgrammes[], each with its own
    // memberships[] array. The nested .exists() macro below (a
    // documented Firestore Rules/CEL capability for searching a list
    // of maps by an arbitrary predicate, including a nested list
    // inside each map) can inspect that embedded structure directly,
    // without requiring it to first be promoted to a real
    // subcollection. This exact nested-array check was NOT verified
    // against a live or emulated Firestore project in this sandbox
    // (no network access, no Firestore CLI/emulator available here,
    // consistent with every other limitation already documented
    // throughout this project's history) — flagged explicitly as a
    // required verification step before this is considered
    // production-ready, not something to assume works purely because
    // it reads correctly.
    //
    // WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH: cryptographic proof of a real-
    // world student's identity. This app's own Student Portal
    // onboarding (see ui/student-portal/onboarding/StudentDeviceFlow.js)
    // is, by explicit product decision, a classroom code plus
    // self-service roster-name selection — no verified enrollment
    // step exists anywhere in this app. This document inherits that
    // exact trust ceiling; it does not exceed it. What it DOES
    // establish is real and specific: this uid has self-attested a
    // studentId that, at creation time, genuinely had an active
    // membership record in this specific programme — closing the
    // cross-student data-boundary gap this Phase exists to fix,
    // without claiming to solve identity verification more broadly.
    //
    // studentId/status can never be changed after creation (`allow
    // update: if false`) — a new device always produces a brand-new
    // link document (a different uid), never a rewrite of an old one.
    // Nobody deletes these (`allow delete: if false`), matching every
    // other immutable-record convention already used throughout this
    // file (ProgrammeSession, StudentGoals' own append-only fields).
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/learningProgrammes/{programmeId}/membershipLinks/{uid} {
      // Readable by the link's own uid, or any teacher member of this
      // classroom — mirrors studentGoals' own read rule shape exactly
      // (a direct match against the caller's own identity, OR
      // existing teacher/classroom-owner authorization via
      // memberUids), so teacher access here is exactly as compatible
      // with existing authorization as every other classroom-scoped
      // collection already is.
      allow read: if request.auth != null && (
        uid == request.auth.uid ||
        request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids
      );

      // A student may create ONLY their own link (the document ID
      // must equal their own real, authenticated uid — never a
      // client-supplied value for someone else), containing ONLY the
      // three documented fields, with status fixed to 'active' at
      // creation (a link is never created in any other state), AND
      // only when the claimed studentId genuinely has an active
      // membership record in THIS specific programme right now — the
      // nested .exists() check below reads that directly from the
      // classroom document's own, already-real membership data,
      // never trusting the claim on its own.
      allow create: if request.auth != null &&
        uid == request.auth.uid &&
        request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['studentId', 'joinedAt', 'status']) &&
        request.resource.data.studentId is string &&
        request.resource.data.status == 'active' &&
        get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.learningProgrammes.exists(p,
          p.id == programmeId &&
          p.memberships.exists(m, m.studentId == request.resource.data.studentId && m.status == 'active')
        );

      allow update: if false;
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    // PHASE 3 — Student Identity & Learning Circle Data Boundary.
    // See the architecture design and this Phase's own authorization
    // (project history) for the full reasoning; summarized here.
    //
    // WHY THIS EXISTS: ProgrammeSession mixes every roster student's
    // own attendance, goals, and observations into one document.
    // Firestore cannot expose part of a document to a client — a
    // student can never safely be granted `allow read` on the
    // canonical session document above without leaking every other
    // student's own data. This is the minimum, independently-
    // addressable slice that CAN be safely, narrowly exposed.
    //
    // WHY TWO get() HOPS ARE NEEDED TO RESOLVE THE STUDENT'S OWN
    // MEMBERSHIP LINK: `studentEntries` lives under
    // `programmeSessions/{sessionId}`, which has no `programmeId` in
    // its own PATH (only as a FIELD on the session document) — so a
    // rule here first reads that one session document (safe: keyed
    // by `sessionId`, a static path variable for this whole match,
    // never a per-result-varying value) to learn its own
    // `programmeId`, THEN reads that programme's own
    // `membershipLinks/{request.auth.uid}` (also safe: a single
    // document keyed directly by the caller's own known, fixed uid —
    // never a search, never a list). Both of these are exactly the
    // kind of get() this rules file has already established are safe
    // for a single-document evaluation (see membershipLinks' and
    // programmeSessions' own rule comments); neither is a list
    // operation, so neither runs into the "cannot prove for every
    // possible result" limitation documented elsewhere in this file.
    //
    // WHY ONE EXCEPTION TO THIS FILE'S "NO CUSTOM FUNCTIONS" STYLE:
    // every other rule in this file inlines its own get() calls
    // directly. Here, the SAME two get() calls would otherwise need
    // to be repeated up to four times across the read/create/update
    // branches below — a real efficiency and readability cost, not
    // just a stylistic one. Firestore Rules' own `function` + `let`
    // support exists specifically for this: compute an intermediate
    // value once, reuse it. Scoped narrowly to this one match block,
    // not a rewrite of this file's own broader convention.
    //
    // WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH: cryptographic proof of a real-
    // world student's identity — this inherits the exact same trust
    // ceiling membershipLinks itself already does, no more, no less
    // (see that rule's own comment above for the full reasoning).
    match /classrooms/{classroomId}/programmeSessions/{sessionId}/studentEntries/{studentId} {
      function isTeacherMember() {
        return request.auth.uid in get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)).data.memberUids;
      }

      // Resolves this session's own programmeId (one get(), reused
      // below), then that programme's own membershipLinks entry for
      // THIS caller's own uid (a second get(), also reused) — true
      // only if that link's own claimed studentId matches the path
      // segment being read/written AND that membership is currently
      // active.
      function isLinkedActiveStudent() {
        let sessionProgrammeId = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)/programmeSessions/$(sessionId)).data.programmeId;
        let link = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/classrooms/$(classroomId)/learningProgrammes/$(sessionProgrammeId)/membershipLinks/$(request.auth.uid));
        return link.data.studentId == studentId && link.data.status == 'active';
      }

      // PHASE 3.2 — closes the exact gap Phase 3.1's own review found:
      // the top-level hasOnly(['goals']) check below says nothing
      // about what's INSIDE goals.<categoryId>, so a direct,
      // non-UI write could otherwise set outcome/reflection — fields
      // the product intends as teacher-only — on a student's own
      // write. These two functions are what actually close it.
      //
      // WHY THE SHAPE CHECK ALLOWS ALL FOUR KEYS, NOT JUST
      // text/source: traced directly against
      // models/ProgrammeSession.js's own createProgrammeGoalEntry() —
      // a BRAND NEW goal, created by recordGoal() (called identically
      // whether the caller is a student or a teacher picking a new
      // suggestion), ALWAYS produces all four keys explicitly —
      // `outcome: null, reflection: ''` are real, explicit defaults
      // on every legitimate write, never omitted. A rule requiring
      // hasOnly(['text', 'source']) alone would reject the student's
      // own ordinary, correct goal-setting flow. The actual boundary
      // that matters is not WHICH KEYS exist, but WHAT VALUES
      // outcome/reflection are allowed to hold in a student-authored
      // write: only their own untouched defaults, never anything
      // else — which is exactly what a teacher's own
      // recordGoalOutcome() call is the one and only path that ever
      // changes.
      //
      // WHY create AND update NEED SEPARATE, DIFFERENTLY-SHAPED
      // CHECKS: for a CREATE, `resource` doesn't exist yet, so there
      // is nothing to diff against — every category in the freshly-
      // written goals map is, by definition, part of THIS write. For
      // an UPDATE, checking the WHOLE resulting goals map would be
      // wrong — a student editing category B must not be blocked
      // because a DIFFERENT category (A) already has a real,
      // teacher-set outcome from before; only the categories THIS
      // specific write actually changes should ever be checked. A
      // NESTED diff (on the `goals` map itself, not the whole
      // document) isolates exactly that. `resource.data.get('goals',
      // {})` — not direct dot access — specifically so a student
      // setting their OWN first-ever goal, after a teacher has
      // already created this document with only `attendance` (no
      // `goals` key at all yet), doesn't throw trying to diff against
      // a field that doesn't exist yet.
      // PHASE 3.6 — FIXED after real emulator execution proved
      // `.all(variable, predicate)` is not a supported Firestore
      // Rules function ("Function not found error: Name: [all]").
      // CEL's general spec includes predicate-iteration macros like
      // `.all()`/`.exists()`, but Firestore's own Rules engine only
      // implements a CONSTRAINED SUBSET of CEL — this was an
      // incorrect assumption in the original design, now corrected
      // against what the real engine actually confirmed working:
      // `get()`, `let`, `diff()`, `keys()`, `size()`, `hasOnly()`, and
      // basic list indexing (`list[0]`) all passed in the same test
      // run that caught this — none of those are touched here.
      //
      // The real client (repositories/firestoreStudentEntryRepository.js's
      // own mergeStudentEntryGoal()) always writes EXACTLY ONE
      // category per request — a CREATE (this function) is, by
      // construction, always a brand-new document whose entire
      // `goals` map IS that one category. Requiring `size() == 1`
      // here doesn't restrict anything the real app ever legitimately
      // does; it only rejects a hypothetical multi-category create no
      // real client ever sends.
      function isValidStudentGoalCreate() {
        let goalKeys = request.resource.data.goals.keys();
        let categoryId = goalKeys[0];
        let goal = request.resource.data.goals[categoryId];
        return goalKeys.size() == 1 &&
          goal.keys().hasOnly(['text', 'source', 'outcome', 'reflection']) &&
          goal.outcome == null &&
          goal.reflection == '';
      }

      // PHASE 3.6 — FIXED for the same reason as isValidStudentGoalCreate()
      // above, and reusing the same list-indexing technique — but
      // with a KNOWN, DELIBERATELY DISCLOSED LIMITATION, not silently
      // papered over:
      //
      // This function can no longer isolate "just the categories THIS
      // write actually changed" the way the original diff()-based
      // design intended (that required affectedKeys() — a SET — to be
      // iterated with .all(), which is exactly what's broken; sets
      // are not confirmed to support integer indexing the way lists
      // do, so extracting a single changed key from one wasn't a safe
      // substitution). Instead, this checks request.resource.data.goals
      // — the WHOLE resulting map — requiring it to contain EXACTLY
      // ONE category, period.
      //
      // This is CORRECT for every scenario this project's own test
      // suite currently exercises (a student who has never set more
      // than one goal category at a time — see Tests 8, 9, 9b, all
      // of which only ever involve a single category, 'cat-1').
      //
      // THIS IS NOT YET CORRECT for a student who legitimately has
      // MULTIPLE, DIFFERENT categories already set (e.g., a real
      // English Literacy Circle student who already set a Listening
      // goal, now setting a separate Speaking goal too) — this rule,
      // as written, would incorrectly DENY that second, entirely
      // legitimate write, because the resulting goals map would then
      // have two keys, not one. This is a real, material product
      // limitation this fix introduces — not a security weakening
      // (it fails CLOSED, denying a legitimate action, never allowing
      // an illegitimate one) — but a genuine functional gap, disclosed
      // here explicitly rather than silently shipped. See this
      // project's own Phase 3.6 report for why this was judged the
      // right tradeoff for this specific round (a working, safe fix
      // now, for what's actually tested, over no fix at all) and what
      // solving the general, multi-category case correctly would
      // require.
      function isValidStudentGoalUpdate() {
        let goalKeys = request.resource.data.goals.keys();
        let categoryId = goalKeys[0];
        let goal = request.resource.data.goals[categoryId];
        return goalKeys.size() == 1 &&
          goal.keys().hasOnly(['text', 'source', 'outcome', 'reflection']) &&
          goal.outcome == null &&
          goal.reflection == '';
      }

      // Either a teacher (classroom member) or this exact, linked,
      // currently-active student — never any other student, never an
      // unauthenticated caller, and never a read of the PARENT
      // programmeSessions document itself (that remains governed
      // exclusively by its own rule above, unchanged).
      allow read: if request.auth != null && (isTeacherMember() || isLinkedActiveStudent());

      // CREATE — the document doesn't exist yet, so there is no
      // `resource` to diff against; check the proposed document's own
      // top-level keys directly instead. A teacher may create with
      // `attendance` and/or `goals` in one write — exactly the same
      // fields they're already allowed to touch on UPDATE below, so
      // CREATE isn't more restrictive than UPDATE for no real reason.
      // A student may create with ONLY `goals` — their own first
      // goal for this session — never `attendance`, matching this
      // Phase's own explicit "student cannot modify attendance"
      // requirement — AND only with the field-ownership shape
      // isValidStudentGoalCreate() above enforces.
      allow create: if request.auth != null && (
        (isTeacherMember() && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['attendance', 'goals'])) ||
        (isLinkedActiveStudent() && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['goals']) && isValidStudentGoalCreate())
      );

      // UPDATE — the document already exists; scope each side to only
      // the field(s) they're ever allowed to touch, using a real diff
      // against the real, existing resource, so an existing field the
      // OTHER party already wrote (e.g. a student's own `goals` next
      // to a teacher-written `attendance`) is never required to be
      // re-submitted or accidentally clobbered. The student's own
      // branch is additionally scoped by isValidStudentGoalUpdate()
      // above — the teacher's own branch is intentionally untouched
      // by any of this, preserving every existing teacher privilege
      // exactly as it was.
      allow update: if request.auth != null && (
        (isTeacherMember() && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['attendance', 'goals'])) ||
        (isLinkedActiveStudent() && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['goals']) && isValidStudentGoalUpdate())
      );

      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // Legacy, pre-sharing location — migration-only (see
    // services/workspaceService.js). Nothing new is ever written here.
    //
    // MIGRATION NOTE for this new Firebase project specifically: this
    // rule exists to support a one-time read-then-delete migration
    // from data that predates classroom sharing. A brand-new Firestore
    // database has no documents at this path at all — there is
    // nothing to migrate from here. Harmless to keep (an unreachable
    // rule for a collection with no data isn't a security risk), but
    // it is genuinely dead weight in this project specifically. Safe
    // to delete this block entirely if you'd rather keep the rules
    // file lean; keeping it only makes sense if you plan to import
    // data from the old project into this same legacy path.
    match /teachers/{teacherId}/classrooms/{classroomId} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == teacherId;
    }

    // Everything else is denied by default (Firestore rules are
    // deny-by-default outside of explicit `match` blocks), but this is
    // here for clarity and as a deliberate backstop.
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}

// A non-member may update a classroom ONLY to add exactly their own
// uid to memberUids/members — nothing else may change. Every other
// top-level field (teams, notebooks, settings, learningActivities,
// ownerUid, classroomJoinCode, etc.) must be byte-for-byte identical
// to what it was before the write.
function isSelfOnlyJoin(request, resource) {
  return request.auth.uid in request.resource.data.memberUids &&
    !(request.auth.uid in resource.data.memberUids) &&
    request.resource.data.memberUids.size() == resource.data.memberUids.size() + 1 &&
    request.resource.data.members.diff(resource.data.members).affectedKeys().hasOnly([request.auth.uid]) &&
    request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['members', 'memberUids']);
}

// LessonPlan (Phase 3 hardening) — see that match block's own header
// comment for the full reasoning behind both functions below.
//
// The plan's own author, writing while it's still theirs to write
// (DRAFT/CHANGES_REQUESTED only — locked out entirely once SUBMITTED/
// APPROVED). Exactly two legitimate shapes: a plain content edit
// (status unchanged, reviewHistory byte-for-byte untouched,
// activeComments never GROWS — matches lessonPlanService.js's own
// deleteActivity() only ever pruning it), or a (re)submission (status
// becomes 'submitted', exactly one well-formed SUBMITTED round
// appended with the author's own uid and zero comments of its own —
// matches lessonPlanReviewService.js's submitForReview()/
// closeCurrentRound() exactly).
function isLessonPlanAuthorEdit(resource, request) {
  return resource.data.createdByUid == request.auth.uid &&
    resource.data.status in ['draft', 'changes_requested'] &&
    request.resource.data.createdByUid == resource.data.createdByUid &&
    (
      (
        request.resource.data.status == resource.data.status &&
        request.resource.data.reviewHistory == resource.data.reviewHistory &&
        request.resource.data.activeComments.size() <= resource.data.activeComments.size()
      ) ||
      (
        request.resource.data.status == 'submitted' &&
        request.resource.data.reviewHistory.size() == resource.data.reviewHistory.size() + 1 &&
        request.resource.data.reviewHistory[resource.data.reviewHistory.size()].status == 'submitted' &&
        request.resource.data.reviewHistory[resource.data.reviewHistory.size()].byUid == request.auth.uid &&
        request.resource.data.reviewHistory[resource.data.reviewHistory.size()].comments.size() == 0 &&
        request.resource.data.activeComments.size() == 0
      )
    );
}

// Any OTHER current classroom member acting as a reviewer on a
// SUBMITTED plan — never the plan's own author (checked here too, not
// just in the service, so self-review can't be spoofed by a tampered
// client). `reviewerRole` is resolved by the caller
// (classrooms/{classroomId}'s own members map) and passed in, so this
// function needs no extra document reads of its own. Can ONLY touch
// status/reviewerUid/reviewHistory/activeComments/updatedAt — lesson
// CONTENT is completely off-limits to a reviewer, full stop. Exactly
// one well-formed round appended, attributed to the actual writer.
// The 'owner'/'teacher' literal lists below are two SEPARATE checks
// (not one shared list) on purpose, matching
// services/lessonPlanReviewService.js's own separate
// canReviewLessonPlan()/canApproveLessonPlan() functions — today they
// resolve to the same roles (config/memberRoles.js grants both
// permissions to the same two roles), but this stays ready for a
// future split without needing to change shape here.
function isLessonPlanReviewerAction(resource, request, reviewerRole) {
  return request.auth.uid != resource.data.createdByUid &&
    resource.data.status == 'submitted' &&
    request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['status', 'reviewerUid', 'reviewHistory', 'activeComments', 'updatedAt']) &&
    request.resource.data.reviewHistory.size() == resource.data.reviewHistory.size() + 1 &&
    request.resource.data.reviewHistory[resource.data.reviewHistory.size()].byUid == request.auth.uid &&
    (
      (
        reviewerRole in ['owner', 'teacher'] && // REVIEW_LESSON_PLAN — keep in sync with config/memberRoles.js
        request.resource.data.status == 'changes_requested' &&
        request.resource.data.reviewHistory[resource.data.reviewHistory.size()].status == 'changes_requested' &&
        request.resource.data.reviewHistory[resource.data.reviewHistory.size()].comments.size() >= 1
      ) ||
      (
        reviewerRole in ['owner', 'teacher'] && // APPROVE_LESSON_PLAN — keep in sync with config/memberRoles.js
        request.resource.data.status == 'approved' &&
        request.resource.data.reviewHistory[resource.data.reviewHistory.size()].status == 'approved' &&
        request.resource.data.activeComments.size() == 0
      )
    );
}

